Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.111SC, Fall 2014 (@gc-mit111: 0, is #reference, #part-of @gc-sources) { r_312Z44VT67CCX60KG27PWM6NZ8
Catherine Drennan. Principles of Chemical Science, MIT 5.111SC, Fall 2014. MIT OpenCourseWare. [Complete course](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/) · [Syllabus](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/pages/syllabus/) · [Exams and solutions](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/resources/exams/).

Work the complete course in sequence after the opening quantitative preparation: lectures, notes, concept questions, problems, and independently attempted exams. The five units develop atoms; bonding/structure; thermodynamics/equilibrium; transition metals/redox; and kinetics. Attempt a problem before consulting its supplied solution. The course is an undergraduate benchmark, not graduate research by itself.

The original campus syllabus assigns Atkins and Jones, Chemical Principles, 5th edition, with 4th-edition references also supplied. That commercial book is not included with OCW; use the openly accessible readings here for the corresponding concepts or obtain an authorized copy independently. Preserve MIT OCW's stated Creative Commons and third-party notices. Instructor, date, available resources, syllabus, and access checked on 29 October 2026. No course media are bundled.

**Return:** [General chemistry sources — books, courses, data, and research](../Sources.lingua#r_37CHT3QSMD33JDMXE59S6M2ME3).
